    "findingId": "F1",
    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "adjustedSeverity": "BUG",
    "reasoning": "The mechanism is confirmed. `message` (routes.ts:47-70) checks only byte length (L49) and text-ness (L53), then pushes the raw frame into `history` (L63) and rebroadcasts it (L68-69); nothing validates the `{userId, text}` envelope. `open` (L41-46) replays every entry to each new socket, and ChatWidget.svelte:52-55 does a bare `JSON.parse(e.data)` then dereferences `msg.text`/`msg.userId` with no try/catch, so `boom` throws SyntaxError and `null` throws TypeError in every receiving browser. I verified there is no XSS: the template renders `{msg.text}` (escaped by Svelte, no `{@html}`). I am downgrading severity because two of the finding's impact claims overstate reality. First, an exception inside an `addEventListener` callback does not remove the listener or break the socket — subsequent valid frames still render, so there is no persistent 'parser crash', only one console error per bad frame. Second, the poisoning is not permanent: `history` is FIFO-bounded to 100 entries / 64 KB (L65-67), so garbage is evicted by ordinary traffic, and a process restart clears it. The net harm is therefore console noise plus blank bubbles occupying history slots on a public demo widget — a real missing-input-validation defect at a trust boundary the server is positioned to enforce (and the same class the maintainers already fixed for this file in #332/#347), but no confidentiality or meaningful integrity impact, so BUG rather than a security MEDIUM. The recommended fix (parse in try/catch, require a bounded `{userId, text}`, store and broadcast the canonical re-serialized form) is correct."
