    "findingId": "F3",
    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "reasoning": "Every link in the chain checks out. `KIND_POLICY` in runtime/requestSetup.ts:66 sets `ws: { csrf: false, ... }`, so `csrfCheck` is skipped for every ws route (requestSetup.ts:119-126 is gated on `policy.csrf`); `Mochi.ws<T>(handlers)` (Mochi.ts:227-236) accepts no config object at all, so there is no `csrf` or `rateLimit` option; and Mochi.ts:1290-1305 shows `upgrade()` returning `false` is the only available rejection path — the chat's `upgrade` (routes.ts:38-40) only derives a rate key and never reads `Origin`. Bun's `server.upgrade()` performs no origin validation. WebSocket handshakes are not subject to the same-origin policy and are not preflighted, so any third-party page a visitor loads can open `wss://mochi.fast/ws/chat`, receive the replayed history, and write into the shared buffer from the victim's IP. The concrete security consequence is the one the finding names: `rateKeyFor()` keys the 20-messages/10s window on `getClientAddress()`, which behind the site's nginx (`proxy: { addressHeader: 'x-forwarded-for', xffDepth: 1 }`) resolves to the real visitor IP — so distributing writes across drive-by visitors defeats the only control on the buffer, and the content is attributed to victims' addresses upstream. I am keeping MEDIUM: this is a missing cross-origin control (CWE-1385) that bypasses another security control, and #349's own commit message states that `upgrade` 'is exactly where auth and client identity belong', confirming the hook exists and is simply unused. I note the classic CSWSH harm — cross-origin reading of cookie-authenticated data — does not apply, since the socket carries no session and the history is already public, which is why this is MEDIUM and not higher."
  },
