    "reasoning": "The code pattern is exactly as described, but the interleaving it requires cannot occur on this codebase's Bun. Confirmed facts: `rateLimitMemoryStore()` returns hitlimit's `MemoryStore` unwrapped (rateLimit.ts:123-125); `MemoryStore.hit()` is synchronous and returns `this._result`, a single object allocated once per store and overwritten on every call (dist/index.js:8, 40-48); and `const { count } = await limiter.hit(...)` reads it one microtask after the call. Two things kill exploitability. (1) Store isolation: the chat's `limiter` is a dedicated instance created at module scope (routes.ts:31), and I grepped the whole repo — routes.ts:57 is the only `.hit()` call against it. Every framework limiter allocates its own store (`options.store ?? rateLimitMemoryStore()`, rateLimit.ts:207), so no HTTP route, no other demo, and no framework `checkLimit` path can touch this `_result`. The only possible interleaving is chat-message-to-chat-message. (2) Bun's event loop forecloses that: the Bun v1.0.26 release notes state 'we've made some infrastructure tweaks to ensure that microtasks are always drained after event loop callbacks are executed. There was a bug where we skipped doing this in websockets…'. This repo pins Bun 1.4.4 (.bun-version, Dockerfile ARG BUN_IMAGE), far past that fix, so the resumption microtask that reads `_result` runs to completion before any other websocket callback can invoke `hit()` again. Mochi's dispatcher (Mochi.ts:1996) discards the handler's promise, but that only means it does not await — it does not let two callbacks share one drain window. Neither the bypass nor the over-block case is reachable. The latent shared-mutable-return hazard is genuine and the suggested snapshot/wrapper fix remains worthwhile defensive hygiene (it would matter if a future store were truly async or shared across routes), which is why I note BUG rather than dismissing it outright — but there is no rate-limit bypass today."
