  {
    "findingId": "F6",
    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "reasoning": "All three framework claims are confirmed by source. (1) `checkRouteLimit` is called in exactly two places — Mochi.ts:981 (page) and Mochi.ts:1211 (api); the ws branch (Mochi.ts:1258+) and sse branch (Mochi.ts:1332+) never call it, and `resolveLimiter` is likewise only invoked in the page/api registration branches, so the global `rateLimit` serve option cannot reach these routes either. `Mochi.ws(handlers)` and `Mochi.sse(handler)` (Mochi.ts:227-236) take no config, unlike `page`/`api` which accept `{ rateLimit }`. (2) Neither branch invokes `middleware`, so no `sequence()` handle can gate them — the site's whole `handle` chain is bypassed. (3) `KIND_POLICY.sse.timeout === true` (requestSetup.ts:65) runs `server.timeout(req, 0)` (requestSetup.ts:85-87); the framework's own docs confirm the intent — 'Mochi already disables the idle timer for page renders and SSE streams' (packages/docs/160-serve-options.md:128). I also verified `MochiSseStream.send` calls `controller.enqueue(frame)` unconditionally with no `desiredSize` check (Mochi.ts:1398), and the stream is constructed with the default count-based queuing strategy, so a client that holds the connection open without reading grows the per-stream queue without bound while the disabled idle timer guarantees the socket never expires; `/ws/time` has the same shape via `ws.send` with no return-value check and no `drain`, and `websocket: { idleTimeout: 30 }` cannot help because the server writes every second. Unlike F5's per-request CPU, these are persistent per-connection resources — one 1 Hz timer, one never-expiring socket and one monotonically growing queue each — with no cap at any layer and no route-level knob to add one, which is the same 'unbounded resource growth on public demo routes' class commit 1289d8f8 treated as a security fix for the chat and HN caches. MEDIUM stands. The recommendations (a module-scope connection ceiling keyed on `getClientAddress()`, 503 before constructing the stream, `upgrade()` returning false for ws, and a `desiredSize` check in `MochiSseStream.send`) are all sound; note `upgrade` is confirmed to be the one callback with a working `getRequestContext()` (Mochi.ts:1294-1297)."
  },
  {
    "findingId": "F7",
    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "reasoning": "Confirmed in full. `intervals` is a `WeakMap` created inside the IIFE at routes.ts:7, so it belongs to one module evaluation. On a dev reload, devWatcher rebuilds the entry and calls `extractServeOptions(outFile, { fresh: true })` (devWatcher.ts:340), re-evaluating the whole first-party graph — a brand-new empty WeakMap and new closures — then hot-swaps via `wsHandlersMap.set(pattern, handler.handlers)` (devWatcher.ts:517). Mochi's Bun `close` handler reads the live map at dispatch time: `wsHandlersMap.get(ws.data.__mochiRoutePattern)?.close?.(ws, code, reason)` (Mochi.ts:2004), so a socket opened before the reload — whose handle lives only in the discarded WeakMap — hits `clearInterval(intervals.get(ws))` with `undefined`, a silent no-op. I also verified the framework passes Bun's `ServerWebSocket` straight through to `open`/`close` without wrapping, so the WeakMap keying is correct in the steady state and only the reload boundary breaks it, exactly as the finding states. The orphaned interval's closure holds `ws` strongly, so neither the timer nor the socket can be collected, and it keeps calling `ws.send` on a dead socket once a second for the life of the process. This fires on essentially every save with the demo tab open, because `applyRouteChanges` swaps the handlers before the browser is told to reload and close its socket. Notably the framework documents this exact hazard class — packages/docs/170-development-mode.md warns that each reload re-creates module-scoped singletons holding an OS resource (it names `setInterval` specifically) and prescribes `pinGlobal`, with a `recompile:module-churn` event firing after ten reloads — so this demo simply does not follow the project's own guidance. Dev-only, self-limited to one dev session, hence BUG; the recommended fixes (stash the handle on `ws.data.user` via an `upgrade` handler, or `pinGlobal` the map as lib/highlight.server.ts does) are both correct."
  },
  {
    "findingId": "F8",
    "verdict": "false-positive",
    "reasoning": "The varlock internals are reported accurately — `ConfigItem._isSensitive = true` is the class default (env-graph-D-UUzmAA.mjs:5884), `resolveSensitiveSource()` consults `@defaultSensitive` only when the data type has no opinion (L5978-6043), `EnvGraph.overrideValues = originalProcessEnv` in the constructor (L8820) with `isOverridden` resolving a declared key from it (L5662, L5677), and env.ts:40-45 does publish every graph entry gated solely on `item.isSensitive`. But the exposure the finding infers does not exist. First I checked the base data types: `DataType.isSensitive` returns `def.sensitive` (env-source-fingerprint-DPQ2KgPB.mjs:47-49) and no built-in type declares it, so without `@defaultSensitive=false` all four keys would inherit the fail-closed `true` and the demo's entire table would render as masked bullets — the header is the mechanism that makes this demo function, not a security regression, and marking the three keys `@public` instead is a pure style change with identical behaviour. Second, the claimed read-back channel into the deployed environment is closed: only schema-declared keys exist in `configSchema`, `overrideValues` is consulted per-declared-item, and I confirmed there is no `.env`/`.env.local` anywhere under packages/site (the only env file in the whole package is this schema), so the renderable set is permanently the four hardcoded `DEMO_*` names — none of which the production process sets, and all of which are placeholder values committed to git. An attacker cannot introduce a key, because the schema is a static file in the image. Third, the one genuinely sensitive key is explicitly `@sensitive` and is masked, which the finding itself concedes. What remains is 'a future maintainer might add a key and forget `@sensitive`' — a hardening suggestion about demo documentation, not a present defect — which lands squarely in the project's documented 'judge packages/site/src/demos/* as documentation, not product' rule. The defence-in-depth recommendation (project an explicit allowlist in `loadVarlockConfig`) is reasonable but fixes nothing exploitable."
  },
  {
    "findingId": "F9",
    "verdict": "true-positive",
    "reasoning": "The code defect is exactly as described and I confirmed it line by line: `cached` is assigned only at env.ts:47, after `await load()` at L34, while the guard reads it at L30 — so there is no in-flight promise to join and no negative caching. Every request arriving inside the first load's window starts its own `load()`, and `load()` is genuinely heavy and side-effecting: `loadVarlockEnvGraph()` → `loadEnvGraph()` constructs a disk-backed `CacheStore`, probes the local-encrypt backend, parses and resolves the graph, then `load()` writes `process.env.__VARLOCK_ENV` and calls `initVarlockEnv()`, which deletes previously-injected keys and rewrites `envState.originalProcessEnv` back over `process.env` (env-CeXQINKg.mjs:547-556). The error half is the substantive part and it is correct: `checkForSchemaErrors`/`checkForConfigErrors` throw, `cached` stays null forever, and every subsequent request re-runs the whole load and re-prints the per-item error block to stderr — an unauthenticated, unbounded retry-and-log loop on a public page with no self-limiting, reachable if a deployed environment ever sets `DEMO_SECRET_KEY` to something not matching `startsWith=sk-` or `DEMO_APP_ENV` outside the enum (both resolve from `process.env` via `overrideValues`), or on any transient load failure. Two corrections that do not change the verdict: `initVarlockEnv()` is fully synchronous, so concurrent loads cannot interleave *inside* it and each rewrite is atomic and idempotent — 'interleaved in attacker-controlled order' overstates the hazard to repeated identical rewrites; and the duplicate-load window only exists before the first success, so the concurrency half is a brief thundering herd rather than a standing exposure. BUG is the right severity, and memoizing the promise with an explicit failure policy is the correct fix."
  },
  {
    "findingId": "F10",
    "verdict": "false-positive",
    "reasoning": "The factual observations are all verifiable — packages/site/src/index.ts contains zero varlock references (I read it end to end), the only entry is routes.ts:7 → `serverProps` → `loadVarlockConfig()` → `await load()` at env.ts:34, and the demo's own index.ts does `await load(); patchGlobalConsole();` before `Mochi.serve()` — but neither the 'contradiction' nor the security consequence holds up. On the contradiction: a demo's `index.ts` tab is an illustrative file across this entire codebase, never the real entry. CLAUDE.md makes that an explicit rule ('Demos must reference this file (labelled as index.ts), never the real ./src/index.ts — the real one carries site-wide middleware/branding that would obscure the demo'), so the divergence is the documented convention for every demo, not a defect in this one. The page copy also does not claim the site boots this way: it says 'These values were parsed, coerced, and validated by varlock during this page's SSR render', which is precisely what happens, and the `patchGlobalConsole()` sentence is describing what the index.ts tab teaches. At most the intro prose ('you load() the schema once at boot') is loose for a demo that loads lazily — a wording nit in marketing copy, not a code defect. On the security consequence: the finding concedes the only `@sensitive` value is the committed placeholder `sk-demo-000000000000`, so the absent `patchGlobalConsole()` redacts nothing real and the `process.env` injection plants four `DEMO_*` placeholders that nothing in the site reads. The genuinely questionable behaviour the finding brushes against — a public GET rewriting the long-lived server's `process.env` on the second and later `initVarlockEnv()` call, which restores `originalProcessEnv` wholesale and would clobber any post-boot mutation — is only reachable via the missing memoization, i.e. it is F9's defect, already credited there. Nothing here is independently real and exploitable."
  },
  {
    "findingId": "F11",
    "verdict": "false-positive",
    "reasoning": "The premise that `__VARLOCK_ENV` is an internal transport that is 'not always JSON' is correct, and varlock does prefix-check before parsing (env-CeXQINKg.mjs:517-521), but the specific failure the finding predicts is unreachable here because of call ordering. I read `load()` in full (dist/index.mjs:11-19): it is linear and its second-to-last statement is `process.env.__VARLOCK_ENV = JSON.stringify(envGraph.getSerializedGraph())`, executed *before* `initVarlockEnv()`. So whatever the variable held on entry — a `varlock:v1:` ciphertext under `@encryptInjectedEnv`, or an injected blob from a `varlock freeze` / `_VARLOCK_USE_INJECTED_ENV=1` parent — is unconditionally overwritten with plain JSON by the time env.ts:36 reads it. I verified `getSerializedGraph()` returns an object literal (`{ blobFormatVersion: 1, varlockVersion, basePath, sources, config, settings }`, env-graph-D-UUzmAA.mjs:9252-9260), so its `JSON.stringify` always begins with `{` and can never carry the encrypted prefix. The early-return path the finding cites (`if (opts?.allowFail || globalThis.__varlockEnvReady) return`) leaves the blob untouched, but that blob is already the JSON this code then parses successfully — and `load()` passes no `opts`, so the only way to reach that branch is with a prefix that `load()` has just removed. There is therefore no unhandled `SyntaxError` and no 500 from this line; if anything upstream fails, `load()` itself throws and L36 never runs (which is F9's territory). The secondary claim — that a future `blobFormatVersion` shape drift would silently render a blank table via `graph.config ?? {}` — is hypothetical fragility about an unreleased version, not a current bug. Reading an internal transport instead of the typed `ENV` proxy or `internal.*` accessors is a legitimate robustness/style criticism and the guards suggested are cheap, but no defect is present today."
  }
]
```

**Summary:** 6 true-positives (F1, F3, F5, F6, F7, F9), 5 false-positives (F2, F4, F8, F10, F11).

The strongest findings are **F3** (confirmed missing Origin check — `KIND_POLICY.ws.csrf === false`, `Mochi.ws` exposes no csrf/rateLimit option, and the one available gate, `upgrade()`, goes unused; it bypasses the per-IP message limit) and **F6** (ws/sse genuinely cannot be rate-limited — `checkRouteLimit` is only called on the page/api paths, the `handle` chain is never invoked, and SSE's idle timer is deliberately disabled, so each connection is a permanent 1 Hz timer plus an unbounded `enqueue` queue).

Two notable corrections:

- **F4 is dead on exploitability.** The shared-mutable `_result` is real, but the chat's store is route-private (the only `.hit()` caller in the repo), and Bun v1.0.26 fixed exactly the bug that would have allowed two websocket callbacks to share one microtask-drain window — this repo pins Bun 1.4.4.
- **F11's failure mode cannot fire.** `load()` unconditionally rewrites `__VARLOCK_ENV` with `JSON.stringify(getSerializedGraph())` *before* `initVarlockEnv()`, so the `varlock:v1:` ciphertext the finding relies on is always gone by the time `JSON.parse` runs.

I also downgraded F1 and F5 to BUG: F1's exception doesn't persist (listeners survive, and history is FIFO-evicted), and F5's amplification is modest against a site whose every page is already an unlimited SSR render — though the missing limit is a genuine inconsistency with the identical `skip: req => req.method !== 'POST'` guard the maintainers applied to the queue and reload-form-data demos.
